## Description
A local variable is in scope inside its own initializer. Nutcracker compiles such programs, and they then crash at runtime with Go-level errors or a compiler INTERNAL_ERROR. The spec (§7.11 Local variable declaration statement, https://ballerina.io/spec/lang/master/#local-var-decl-stmt) says: "The scope of variables declared in a local-var-decl-stmt starts immediately after the statement and continues to the end of the statement block in which it occurs." So a reference to the variable inside its own initializer, including inside an anonymous function in the initializer, refers to an undefined symbol and must be a compile error.

## Steps to Reproduce
Typed self-recursive closure: it compiles, then crashes at runtime.
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    function (int) returns int fact = function (int n) returns int {
        return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1); // @error
    };
    io:println(fact(5));
}
```
Actual:
```
error: interface conversion: values.BalValue is nil, not *values.Function
        at main(r2.bal:5)
           main(r2.bal:7)
```

`var` version: the compiler crashes.
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    var fact = function (int n) returns int {
        return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1); // @error
    };
    io:println(fact(5));
}
```
Actual:
```
fatal[INTERNAL_ERROR]: expression type not resolved
 --> r2b.bal:5:38
```

Simplest form:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    int z = z + 1; // @error
    io:println(z);
}
```
Actual: it compiles, then fails with `error: unsupported type combination: <nil> + int64 at main(z.bal:4)`.

Expected for all three: a compile error (undefined symbol) on the self-reference.

## Affected Version(s)
upstream main @ 1bcb85b9

## OS, DB, other environment details and versions
Linux, `go run ./cli/cmd run <file.bal>`

## Related area
Semantics

## Related issue(s) (optional)
Similar runtime symptoms, different causes: #1151 (parameter default reads a captured local), #1322 (module-level function variable called before it is initialized), #1423 (same INTERNAL_ERROR text, different trigger).

## Suggested label(s) (optional)
