## Description
When a type test against a built-in type name (`int`, `string`, ...) is the middle operand of a conditional expression `c ? a : b`, the parser reads `int : ...` as a qualified identifier, even though there is whitespace around the colon. Depending on what follows, this gives a bogus syntax error or crashes the compiler with an INTERNAL_ERROR. With a user-defined type name (`x is T : false`), or with the type test in parentheses (`b ? (x is int) : x is string`), the program works.

Spec: a qualified identifier is `qualified-identifier := module-prefix NoSpaceColon identifier`, and the lexical structure section says: "When NoSpaceColon is used in a production, TokenWhiteSpace is not allowed immediately before or after the colon." So `int : true` cannot be a qualified identifier and must parse as the `:` of the conditional expression (§6.x Conditional expression, https://ballerina.io/spec/lang/master/#conditional-expr).

## Steps to Reproduce
Compiler crash:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

function f(int|string x, boolean b) returns string {
    if b ? x is int : x is string {
        return "yes";
    }
    return "no";
}

public function main() {
    io:println(f(1, true)); // @output yes
}
```
Actual:
```
fatal[INTERNAL_ERROR]: Unexpected builtin type kind
 --> cond-is-builtin2.bal:4:17
  |
4 |     if b ? x is int : x is string {
  |                 ^^^
```

Bogus syntax error:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    int|string x = 1;
    boolean b = true;
    boolean r = b ? x is string : true;
    io:println(r); // @output false
}
```
Actual:
```
error[SYNTAX_ERROR]: missing slash asterisk token
 --> cond-is-builtin1.bal:6:33
```

Expected: both programs compile; they print `yes` and `false`.

## Affected Version(s)
upstream main @ 1bcb85b9

## OS, DB, other environment details and versions
Linux, `go run ./cli/cmd run <file.bal>`

## Related area
Parser

## Related issue(s) (optional)
#1726 (also a parser problem with NoSpaceColon and prefixed names, in a different construct)

## Suggested label(s) (optional)
Type/Bug

The same wraparound also happens on the binary additive path, not only for unary `-`. The static singleton type of `+`/`-` on int-literal operands is computed with two's-complement wraparound, so an addition that always panics with overflow is typed as the wrapped singleton:

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    -9223372036854775808|error r = trap (9223372036854775807 + 1); // @error
    io:println(r is error);
    9223372036854775807|error s = trap (-9223372036854775807 - 2); // @error
    io:println(s is error);
}
```

Actual (main @ 1bcb85b9): compiles and prints `true` twice. The checker types `9223372036854775807 + 1` as the singleton `-9223372036854775808`, and `-9223372036854775807 - 2` as the singleton `9223372036854775807`. At runtime both operations panic, as they should.

Expected: the wrapped value should not be the static type. §6.2.4 says the singleton subtype "is computed by evaluating the expression at compile-time with operands whose values belong to the corresponding singleton types", and for int "+ performs integer addition; a panic will occur on overflow". Evaluating these expressions produces no value, so the singleton refinement should not apply and the type should fall back to `int`. Then the assignments above are compile errors (a plain type mismatch, not a compile-time overflow error, so this is not the case closed in #1492).

This needs its own overflow check in the additive singleton computation (`additiveSingletonType` in `semantics/internal/types/type_resolver.go`) in addition to the one `negateNumericType` needs for this issue.
